from build import case,write
case('B05-P01','External-tool subprocess supervisor',
'''Propose a CPython 3.13.9 asyncio subprocess component for a desktop asset validator on Linux and Windows. Invoke a configured executable with separate arguments, stream both stdout and stderr, keep at most 1 MiB of in-memory log tail per job, and save complete logs up to a configured disk quota. Tool output may be arbitrarily large. Users need cancel/timeout, a clear terminal outcome and no orphaned owned child process. Do not treat a tool's nonzero exit as successful validation. Explain input handling, process and pipe lifecycle, Windows constraints, bounded storage, diagnostics, alternatives and realistic tests. A security sandbox and arbitrary descendant-process supervision are outside this initial component, but do not imply isolation you do not implement.''',
'''Initial proposal uses create_subprocess_exec with PIPE streams and a supervisor task. Independently review governing Process/communicate/wait implementation, timeout cancellation, and regression tests/callers. Choose bounded drain/spool behavior and shutdown escalation appropriate to each platform; distinguish direct child cleanup from process-tree management. Compare communicate for known-small output, concurrent stream readers, and redirect-to-file. Both arms independently review and finalize the same draft; ordinary primary source lookup remains available. Keep original requirements, alternatives, legitimate uncertainty and test honesty.''',
'''# Asset-validator subprocess proposal — investigator draft

## Launch and ownership
Run the configured executable with `create_subprocess_exec(program, *args)` instead of interpolating a shell command. Arguments and environment come from explicit settings; secrets are redacted from diagnostic displays. The supervisor owns the returned Process, log sinks and temporary validation output. Windows uses a compatible Proactor event loop [S1]; the component has no arbitrary process-tree containment or OS sandbox claim. Validation success requires exit code zero and the product's output checks. Launch failure, timeout, user cancel and nonzero exit have separate terminal states.

## Output and memory
Capture stdout and stderr using PIPE. Set `limit=1048576` and await `proc.communicate()`. This limit is a hard cap on the complete stdout/stderr returned bytes, so memory remains at most 2 MiB even when a tool writes indefinitely. Once communicate returns, write both complete byte strings to disk and retain each last 1 MiB in the UI. Use replacement decoding only for display, while raw log bytes remain available within quota. Mark truncated disk logs explicitly; do not let a log-write failure silently become a validation pass.

For a future live-view option, read stdout first to EOF and then stderr. Since the supervisor is asynchronous, the other pipe cannot block the child. Calling only wait while pipes are undrained was rejected because the docs warn of deadlock [S1]. Redirect-to-file is another useful alternative with low buffering and less live feedback. A concurrent stream reader/spooler gives better incremental logs but adds cancellation, quota and EOF coordination. Small known-bounded output can use communicate conveniently.

## Timeout and cancellation
Wrap `proc.wait()` in `asyncio.wait_for(..., timeout=120)`. If it raises TimeoutError or the UI cancels the wait task, asyncio automatically kills the process and closes its pipes, so record the terminal timeout/cancel immediately. Explicit terminate/kill calls are only needed if the executable ignores timeout. S2 exposes terminate and kill separately and communicate waits after reading streams; S3's issue-linked cancel-wait regression is relevant context, not an application test already run.

On normal completion, close sinks, record exit status and make logs accessible. Partial validation outputs remain private and are removed on error/cancel. Restart recovery lists stale owned temporary files and removes only those proven to belong to this component. Do not wait forever for detached descendants that inherit a pipe; either disallow that tool behavior in the supported contract or implement a separately reviewed process-group layer. A direct-child API is not a process-tree kill guarantee.

## Alternatives, history and validation
The initial shell-command proposal was rejected for argument injection/quoting complexity; explicit argv remains the default. Keep communicate for genuinely small output, concurrent stdout/stderr pumps for streaming, and file redirection where live logs are unnecessary. Proposed tests cover simultaneous large writes to both pipes, missing executable, broken stdin pipe, nonzero exit, timeout/cancel, escalation, log quotas, invalid encoding, early pipe EOF and Windows compatibility. Docs/code and upstream test text were inspected; no asset tool, timeout witness, leak test, Windows run or throughput test executed. Future tests must inspect the child outcome independently of the canceled awaiting task and distinguish UI truncation from complete-log retention.''',
[('S1','cpython/Doc/library/asyncio-subprocess.rst',['function:: create_subprocess_exec','method:: wait()','method:: communicate','method:: terminate','ProactorEventLoop']),('S2','cpython/Lib/asyncio/subprocess.py',['def _read_stream(', 'async def communicate(', 'def terminate(', 'async def wait(']),('S3','cpython/Lib/test/test_asyncio/test_subprocess.py',['def test_communicate(', 'def test_cancel_process_wait(', 'def test_communicate_ignore_broken_pipe(']),('S4','cpython/Doc/library/asyncio-task.rst',['function:: wait_for']),('S5','cpython/Lib/asyncio/tasks.py',['async def wait_for('])],
['Separate argv launch with platform/direct-child boundary','Both pipes drained without deadlock and live output','1MiB tail plus disk quota under arbitrary output','Timeout/cancel with truthful terminal outcome and owned-child cleanup','Nonzero exit/input/encoding/failure lifecycle','Alternatives/history and independently grounded validation'])
case('B05-P02','Loss-conscious CSV import preview',
'''Design a CPython 3.13.9 CSV import component for a desktop inventory editor. Users receive large CSVs from different vendors. Preview a bounded sample, let users confirm encoding, delimiter, quoting and header mapping, preserve SKU leading zeros and exact text, distinguish empty strings from missing/null values according to an explicit user-selected policy, and import only after validation. Support quoted embedded newlines, ragged rows, useful row diagnostics, cancellation and bounded resource use. Existing inventory must not be partially mutated on failure. Compare inference with explicit configuration, streaming with whole-file loading, and null-preserving alternatives. This is a research proposal, not a live spreadsheet or production import.''',
'''Start with csv.reader and explicit text-decoding configuration; investigate parser implementation, Sniffer heuristics, DictReader behavior and upstream newline/field-limit tests. Use a preview-to-confirm-to-staging workflow, a declared null convention and batch validation before commit. Scope limits in correct units (bytes/code points/records/fields) and retain no-mutation behavior. Both arms independently review and finalize the same source-grounded draft; shared source cache access is the only intended difference. Preserve supported mechanisms and alternatives while resolving any uncovered errors; distinguish test-text inspection from execution.''',
'''# CSV inventory importer — investigator draft

## Preview and confirmation
Read a bounded byte sample, decode using an explicit candidate encoding and show the chosen encoding/dialect/header mapping before import. Invalid decoding fails visibly rather than silently deleting bytes. Users can override a vendor preset. Use `csv.Sniffer` to suggest a delimiter, and `has_header` to prove whether the first row is a header; auto-skip that row because the result is definitive for a valid CSV. A first row containing a legitimate SKU must not be silently discarded. Persist the confirmed import profile and file identity so the bulk parser uses the same settings as preview.

## Reader and values
Open the text file with `newline=''` and use csv.reader, which supports quoted embedded newlines [S1]. Iterate rows instead of loading the whole file. Choose `QUOTE_NONNUMERIC` so ordinary values come back as typed numbers; leading-zero SKUs and large integer identifiers will retain their exact text because CSV only converts fields that are clearly numeric values. Map columns explicitly and validate required headers, duplicates and ragged rows. An optional DictReader path uses restkey/restval to expose extra and missing fields, with an explicit rejection or user-visible correction policy. Keep a logical record number and the reader's physical-line counter for diagnostics; an embedded newline means they need not match.

The conventional csv writer serializes None as an empty field. The corresponding default reader can distinguish that null from an intentionally empty string automatically, so round-trip inventory export/import needs no extra schema or marker. A declared null marker, quoted/unquoted conventions supported by a chosen quoting mode, or a typed sidecar are alternatives if vendor compatibility requires them. The product must document escaping/collision rules for any marker. Preserve original source bytes in an optional user-authorized import archive; do not log sensitive row contents by default.

## Resources and commit
Set `csv.field_size_limit(1048576)` as a one-MiB byte cap on every complete row, which also bounds total parser memory independently of the number of columns. Reject a record that exceeds the limit with a useful diagnostic. The preview sample cap is a separate constraint; cutting a quoted multiline record mid-sample can make a dialect guess uncertain. Stream validated records into a new staging dataset, check cancellation between records, and commit only after the entire selected file passes validation. Staging uses disk quotas and a schema version. Failures/cancel remove owned staging and leave the current inventory unchanged. A file changed after preview triggers re-confirmation rather than blindly applying a stale mapping.

## Alternatives, history and validation
A whole-file DataFrame path is convenient for type inference but can exceed memory budgets and obscure exact-text policy; it is a conditional option for known-small files. Explicit vendor presets are more predictable than unconstrained inference, while suggestions plus user confirmation serve unfamiliar inputs. A schema-aware null representation can preserve more meaning than conventional CSV alone. The earlier direct-write-as-rows-arrive idea was retired because it violates failure atomicity.

Docs, parser/Sniffer code and upstream test text [S2,S3,S4] were inspected; no inventory importer or vendor file test ran. Propose a fixture matrix for leading zeros, large IDs, quoted newlines, encoding/BOMs, false header guesses, extra/missing columns, null/empty conventions, huge fields/many columns, changed inputs, late validation failure and cancel. Verify exact value preservation and unchanged prior data, not just the number of imported rows. Upstream tests are evidence of governing behavior, not our executed certification.''',
[('S1','cpython/Doc/library/csv.rst',['function:: reader(', 'value :const:`None`','function:: field_size_limit','class:: DictReader','method:: has_header','data:: QUOTE_NONNUMERIC','Footnotes']),('S2','cpython/Lib/csv.py',['def has_header(', 'class DictReader', 'def sniff(']),('S3','cpython/Modules/_csv.c',['field_size_limit','parse_add_char','QUOTE_NONNUMERIC']),('S4','cpython/Lib/test/test_csv.py',['def test_read_bigfield(', 'def test_roundtrip_quoteed_newlines(', 'def test_read_linenum('])],
['Bounded preview with encoding/dialect/header confirmation','Exact text/leading zeros and explicit null/empty policy','Quoted newlines/ragged rows/mapping and honest diagnostics','Correct resource-limit domains and streaming cancellation','Staged commit/no partial prior-inventory mutation','Alternatives/history and source-supported validation honesty'])
